  "status": "unreviewed-fixture"
 },
 "notes": "Libretto by Lorenzo Da Ponte (1787). Words follow the Schirmer vocal score's spelling, punctuation and syllables, and its repeats of words and lines. Where the print differs from the libretto the words follow the print: Leporello “Cosa rara!„ with the print's quotation marks, “piano vo'inghiottir” (vo' and inghiottir on one printed syllable, no elision mark), “Questo poi la conosco” (libretto Questa), “Non sò far”, and “sì eccellente è'l vostro cuoco” (libretto è il); Don Giovanni “Cos' è?” on two notes and “Sì eccellente è il cuoco mio” (the print drops the accent on è, a clear misprint corrected here) and “anch' ei” as two words. The piece ends on the last bar of p. 263: Don Giovanni's and Leporello's “provar” is cut there, its first syllable “pro-” sung in this piece and “-var.” on the first bar of the next (“L'ultima prova dell'amor mio”). The stage band's three tunes (Martín y Soler's Una cosa rara, Sarti's Fra i due litiganti and “Non più andrai” from Mozart's Figaro) are in the piano. IPA, word meanings and translations were drafted for this app (S58) and the IPA was checked against Castel. IPA follows sung Italian conventions: stressed syllable marked, elisions (two words on one note in the score) joined with ‿, open e/o as ɛ/ɔ. Translation literal, for meaning.",
